---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CloudflareOperatingDiagram from "../components/CloudflareOperatingDiagram.astro";
import { ArrowRight, Database, Globe2 } from "@lucide/astro";

const repository = "https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer";
const evidence = `${repository}/blob/main/docs/evidence/cloudflare-review-20261112/README.md`;
const baselime =
  "https://blog.cloudflare.com/80-percent-lower-cloud-cost-how-baselime-moved-from-aws-to-cloudflare/";
const workersPricing =
  "https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/";
const d1Pricing = "https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/pricing/";
const accessDocs =
  "https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/access-controls/applications/http-apps/";
---
<Base
  title="Build your app, not your infrastructure"
  description="A Cloudflare-first operating model for App Composer: the resources the Creator Hub reference actually runs, its measured cost at real volume, the operational work it removes and the limits we do not hide."
  ogType="article"
>
  <div class="cost-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Cloudflare-first, measured</p>
    <h1>Build your app,<br />not your infrastructure.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The supported Creator Hub reference runs on two Workers, two D1
      databases and one R2 bucket. There is no server to patch, no database
      host to run and no separate CDN to configure. The owner still controls
      the source, the data and every release decision.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/creator-hub">
        See the live reference <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true" />
      </a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/ready">Prepare your environment</a>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      Observed 12 October–12 November 2026 in the operator’s Cloudflare
      account. This page separates Cloudflare’s published precedent, our
      measured reference and illustrative growth assumptions. It is not a
      Cloudflare partnership, endorsement or official integration.
    </p>
  </div>

  <nav class="platform-index" aria-label="Cost and operations sections">
    <a href="#what-runs">What runs</a>
    <a href="#baselime">The Baselime precedent</a>
    <a href="#reference">Our measured reference</a>
    <a href="#growth">What changes with scale</a>
    <a href="#operations">What you stop operating</a>
    <a href="#limits">Limits and claims</a>
  </nav>

  <section class="cost-section" id="what-runs">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The supported shape</p>
        <h2>Fewer moving parts, with clear ownership.</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">One scenario · measured in production</span>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">
      The Creator Hub reference is two independently owned applications. Here /
      there receives and moderates contributions; Linkarooie curates the public
      profile and reads approved items through a service binding. Each app has
      its own Worker, source repository and database. Only Here / there stores
      uploaded media.
    </p>
    <CloudflareOperatingDiagram />
    <div class="fact-strip" aria-label="Reference resource counts">
      <div><strong>2</strong><span>Workers</span></div>
      <div><strong>2</strong><span>D1 databases</span></div>
      <div><strong>1</strong><span>R2 bucket</span></div>
      <div><strong>1</strong><span>service binding</span></div>
      <div><strong>1</strong><span>daily cron</span></div>
      <div><strong>2</strong><span>custom hostnames</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      There are no Queues, Workflows, Durable Objects or KV namespaces in this
      pair. The only scheduled work is Here / there’s existing daily
      maintenance trigger. Both apps are production deployments; the reference
      is operator-owned, not a customer-owned onboarding proof.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="baselime">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Externally sourced precedent</p>
        <h2>What Cloudflare measured at another company.</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">Baselime · Cloudflare estimate</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      Cloudflare’s published Baselime case study describes a much larger
      observability workload moving from AWS to Cloudflare. The numbers below
      are Cloudflare’s estimate for a comparable paying customer, not an
      independently audited saving and not an App Composer result. Baselime was
      acquired by Cloudflare in 2024.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Baselime’s reported daily infrastructure estimate (USD)</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Workload</th><th scope="col">AWS before</th><th scope="col">Cloudflare estimate</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Compute</td><td>$650 Lambda</td><td>$25 Workers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CDN</td><td>$140 CloudFront</td><td>$0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Streaming and analytics</td><td>$1,150 Kinesis / EC2</td><td>$300 Workers Analytics Engine</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Total daily</strong></td><td><strong>$1,940</strong></td><td><strong>$325</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Annualised</strong></td><td><strong>$708,100</strong></td><td><strong>$118,625</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="attribution">
      Reported difference: roughly <strong>83% lower estimated infrastructure
      cost</strong>, about <strong>$589,475 per year</strong> in this case.
      Source:
      <a href={baselime}>Cloudflare’s Baselime case study</a>, published 31
      October 2024.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Do not read this as “83% cheaper with App Composer”. Baselime’s workload
      is not a WordPress site, a hobby app or a typical Creator Hub. Its value
      here is the architectural argument: fewer managed services can remove
      real operating work. Our own scenario is measured separately below.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="reference">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">App Composer’s own observed result</p>
        <h2>The Creator Hub reference, at real volume.</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">12 Oct–12 Nov 2026 · 31 days</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      This is the supported two-app reference at
      <a href="https://creator-hub.loftwah.com">creator-hub.loftwah.com</a> and
      <a href="https://creator-hub-guestbook.loftwah.com">creator-hub-guestbook.loftwah.com</a>.
      The readings come from Cloudflare’s Workers and D1 analytics for the
      production scripts, plus the live D1 and R2 metadata. The source
      revisions served during the window were
      <code>b7cd3b3</code> for Linkarooie and <code>36797b8</code> for Here /
      there.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Observed reference usage and the applicable Cloudflare allowance</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Observed</th><th scope="col">Included on Workers Paid</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Worker requests</td><td>1,660 across both apps</td><td>10,000,000 per month</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D1 rows read</td><td>2,841</td><td>25,000,000,000 per month</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D1 rows written</td><td>138</td><td>50,000,000 per month</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D1 stored data</td><td>249,856 bytes (about 0.25 MB)</td><td>5 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>R2 stored data</td><td>2 objects, about 0.12 MB</td><td>10 GB-month free</td></tr>
          <tr><td>R2 egress</td><td>Not separately measured</td><td>Free</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scheduled invocations</td><td>One daily maintenance trigger</td><td>Billed as Worker requests</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="cost-callout">
      <Database size={24} aria-hidden="true" />
      <div>
        <h3>What this cost</h3>
        <p>
          At the observed request and storage volumes, the usage attributable
          to this pair is <strong>$0 above the account’s US$5 monthly Workers
          Paid minimum</strong>. We do not claim the pair costs $5 in
          isolation: the account also contains App Composer and other
          services, and Cloudflare bills the account minimum once.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      Excluded from this comparison: domain registration and renewal,
      Cloudflare plans beyond Workers Paid, Cloudflare Access / Zero Trust
      seats, agent or model usage, developer and maintenance labour, taxes,
      backups, support and every other resource in the account. A different
      app, traffic pattern or retention policy can change the result. The
      source data and commands are in the
      <a href={evidence}>review evidence</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="growth">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Assumption-labelled model</p>
        <h2>What changes as it grows.</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">Workers only · D1/R2 excluded</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      Cloudflare’s current Workers Paid plan includes 10 million requests and
      30 million CPU milliseconds per month. The rows below use Cloudflare’s
      own published examples for request and CPU charges. They do not include
      D1, R2, Access, domains or agent usage, so they are a lower bound rather
      than a whole-project quote.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Illustrative Workers charges under the current published price schedule</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Monthly assumption</th><th scope="col">Requests</th><th scope="col">CPU per request</th><th scope="col">Workers charge</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Observed reference</td><td>1,660</td><td>Not measured</td><td>$0 usage; $5 account minimum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Small supported app</td><td>1,000,000</td><td>10 ms</td><td>$0 usage; $5 account minimum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloudflare’s 15M example</td><td>15,000,000</td><td>7 ms</td><td>$8.00 including the $5 minimum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloudflare’s 100M example</td><td>100,000,000</td><td>7 ms</td><td>$45.40 including the $5 minimum</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      The 1M row is our assumption, not a measured run. The 15M and 100M rows
      reproduce Cloudflare’s published pricing examples. At higher volume, add
      D1 rows and storage, R2 operations and storage, Access seats and any
      other product you choose. Current sources:
      <a href={workersPricing}>Workers pricing</a> and
      <a href={d1Pricing}>D1 pricing</a>, reviewed 12 November 2026.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="operations">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The operating difference</p>
        <h2>Spend the time on the product.</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">Responsibility comparison</span>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Who performs each operating job in the two models</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Job</th><th scope="col">Typical self-managed stack</th><th scope="col">Creator Hub reference</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Runtime</td><td>Provision and patch a VM or container host</td><td>Cloudflare runs the Workers runtime</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Database</td><td>Run the database server, backups and failover</td><td>D1, with migrations committed to source</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Media</td><td>Bucket, CDN and credential rotation</td><td>R2 behind a Worker binding, free egress</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Admin access</td><td>VPN, SSO proxy or IP allowlist</td><td>Cloudflare Access in front of native EmDash</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Background work</td><td>Queue, worker pool and scheduler host</td><td>One existing Cron Trigger; no queue in this pair</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Deployment</td><td>Build host, release scripts and rollback procedure</td><td>Guarded repository release with an exact Worker version</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Observation</td><td>Install and maintain an observability stack</td><td>Workers observability, <code>/healthz</code> and release receipts</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="platform-columns">
      <div>
        <h3>What the owner still does</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Owns the GitHub repositories and reviews source changes.</li>
          <li>Approves contributions before they become public.</li>
          <li>Holds the Cloudflare account, domain, Access policy and owner credentials.</li>
          <li>Chooses when to deploy and checks the live result.</li>
          <li>Decides retention, backups and what data may be exported.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What Cloudflare operates</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>The Workers runtime, D1, R2, KV and the network path.</li>
          <li>The custom-domain certificate and edge routing.</li>
          <li>The scheduled trigger’s invocation.</li>
          <li>Access enforcement at the edge.</li>
          <li>Usage metering and the published service limits.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cost-section" id="limits">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Claim discipline</p>
        <h2>What this page does not claim.</h2>
      </div>
      <span class="evidence-label">Known limits stay visible</span>
    </div>
    <div class="platform-columns">
      <div>
        <h3>Supported and verified</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Two independently owned Workers, two D1 databases and one R2 bucket in production.</li>
          <li>One service binding, two custom hostnames and one existing daily Cron Trigger.</li>
          <li>Cloudflare Access in front of native administration; anonymous requests refuse.</li>
          <li>A guarded production release with exact source and Worker identity.</li>
          <li>Preview isolation: distinct D1/R2/KV, no services, no routes and no cron triggers.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Unverified or unsupported</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>No universal saving, “zero infrastructure cost” or automatically lower bill.</li>
          <li>No Cloudflare partnership, endorsement or official integration.</li>
          <li>No customer-owned onboarding, physical-device acceptance or external participant proof.</li>
          <li>No authenticated agent/MCP journey in this review, and no hosted Get Ready API.</li>
          <li>No claim that every workload fits Workers, D1 or R2.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="risk-list">
      <h3>Next three technical risks</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Preview is a public, stale operator deployment.</strong>
          It has isolated data and no production services, routes or cron, but it
          still answers on <code>workers.dev</code>. Refresh or retire it
          deliberately rather than treating it as a sandbox.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>88 undeclared Worker scripts remain in the account.</strong>
          Their retention, traffic and rollback role are unverified. No deletion
          is safe from a name or tag alone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The exact-source gate is conservative.</strong>
          A docs-only commit on a maintained app makes its live
          <code>/build.json</code> stale until the app is redeployed. That is
          safe, but it means readiness can refuse a runtime-identical release.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      Technical review pack:
      <a href={evidence}>Cloudflare review, 12 November 2026</a>. Release
      history: <a href="/release">verified outcomes</a>. Access model:
      <a href={accessDocs}>Cloudflare Access documentation</a>. Architecture:
      <a href="/architecture">how App Composer fits together</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="cost-close">
    <Globe2 size={28} aria-hidden="true" />
    <div>
      <h2>Start with the result you want.</h2>
      <p>
        Get Ready establishes the supported environment before asking for an
        app idea. The Creator Hub is a real, inspectable example of what comes
        next.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/ready">Open Get Ready</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/creator-hub">Inspect the reference</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .cost-intro {
    max-width: 940px;
  }
  .cost-intro h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 5.8vw, 5.2rem);
    max-width: 20ch;
    margin: 14px 0 24px;
  }
  .cost-intro .lede {
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 1.18rem;
  }
  .cost-intro .small {
    max-width: 76ch;
  }
  .platform-index {
    border-block: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    padding: 16px 0;
    gap: 16px 30px;
    margin: 36px 0;
  }
  .platform-index a {
    min-height: 44px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .cost-section {
    padding: 42px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    scroll-margin-top: 24px;
  }
  .cost-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.6vw, 2.7rem);
    max-width: 27ch;
    margin: 4px 0 18px;
  }
  .cost-section > .lede {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    max-width: 72ch;
  }
  .section-heading {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  .section-heading .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
  }
  .evidence-label {
    flex: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 7px 12px;
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    max-width: 28ch;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .fact-strip {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(6, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--color-border);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    margin: 26px 0 14px;
  }
  .fact-strip div {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding: 16px 14px;
  }
  .fact-strip strong {
    display: block;
    font: 600 1.8rem/1 Space, system-ui, sans-serif;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .fact-strip span {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 6px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
  table caption {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0 0 12px;
    color: var(--color-foreground);
  }
  table th {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-foreground);
  }
  table td,
  table th {
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .attribution {
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding: 16px 18px;
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    max-width: 80ch;
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  .cost-callout {
    display: flex;
    gap: 18px;
    align-items: flex-start;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding: 22px;
    margin: 24px 0 16px;
  }
  .cost-callout svg {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    flex: none;
    margin-top: 2px;
  }
  .cost-callout h3 {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    font-size: 1.3rem;
  }
  .cost-callout p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 74ch;
  }
  .platform-columns {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 48px;
    margin-top: 28px;
  }
  .platform-columns h3,
  .risk-list h3 {
